<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>17: Laplace's equation</title>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose we have a rectangular plate whose dimensions are
  <m>10\times5</m>. Remember that Laplace's equation
  <md>\nabla^2 u =\pdtwo{u}{x} +\pdtwo{u}{y} = 0</md>
  describes the steady state temperature distribution <m>u(x,y)</m> in the
  plate.  Solve Laplace's equation in the rectangle with the boundary
  conditions shown.  In particular, <m>u=0</m> on the bottom, left, and
  right, and <m>u=1</m> on the top.  You should be able to draw the graph
  of the solution as well.</p>
      <p>\begin{minipage}{2.5in}
    {</p>
      <p>\begin{center}
        \includegraphics{figures/16-rect.eps}
      \end{center}</p>
      <p>}
  \end{minipage}
  \begin{minipage}{2.5in}
    {
      <md>
      \begin{aligned}
        \nabla^2 u \amp  = 0 \\
        u(x,0) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(0,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(10,y) \amp  = 0 \\
        u(x,5) \amp  = 1
      \end{aligned}
      </md></p>
      <p>}
  \end{minipage}</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that you have an infinite rectangular plate;  that is,
  the plate is between <m>0\leq x\leq \pi</m> and <m>0\leq y</m>.  Solve Laplace's
  equation in this plate with the boundary conditions
  <md>
<mrow>u(x,0) \amp  = \amp  1</mrow>
<mrow>u(0, y) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(\pi, y) \amp  = \amp  0</mrow>
<mrow>\lim_{y\to\infty}u(x,y)\amp =\amp 0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>How would the solution to the first problem be different if we
  change the  
  boundary conditions?</p>
      <p><md>
  \begin{aligned}
    \nabla^2 u \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x,0) \amp  = 0 \\
    \pd{u}{x}(0,y) \amp  = 0 \\
    \pd{u}{x}(10,y) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x,5) \amp  = 1
  \end{aligned}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
